<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.3.4: Completing the Square with Fractions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02375</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.3.4: Completing the Square with Fractions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>83464ad2-5016-4901-887f-19b6d8aea24d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<example id="exam-00001"><exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00001">The solutions to this equation are \(x = \frac{3}{4}\) and \(x=\frac{1}{4}\). Show how to find those solutions by <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">completing the square</term>. \(x^2−x+\frac{3}{16}=0\)</para>
  
  <para id="para-00002">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00004">\(\begin{array}{rcl}x^2-x+\frac3{16}&amp;=&amp;0\\x^2-x+\frac14&amp;=&amp;-\frac3{16}+\frac14=-\frac3{16}+\frac4{16}=\frac1{16}\\x^2-x+\frac14&amp;=&amp;\frac1{16}\\\sqrt{\left(x-\frac12\right)^2}&amp;=&amp;\sqrt{\frac1{16}}\\x-\frac12&amp;=&amp;\pm\frac14\\x-\frac12&amp;=&amp;\frac14\\x-\frac12&amp;=&amp;-\frac14\end{array}\) \( \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x=\frac{1}{4} \text { and } x=\frac{3}{4}\) </para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
</document>